A.7.5 Multi-model results on U.S. Presidents Ordering Task L95:       6. cite85†A.7.6 Claude 3.7 Sonnet Extended Thinking Trace L96:     8. cite86†A.8 Conditional Sorting L97:       1. cite87†A.8.1 Experimental Design L98:     9. cite88†A.9 Conditional Sorting Results L99:       1. cite89†A.9.1 With LRMs L100:   11. cite90†References L101: cite91†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L102: \nL103: arXiv:2511.14214v2 [cs.AI] 19 Nov 2025\nL104: # Do Large Language Models (LLMs) Understand Chronology?\nL105: \nL106: Pattaraphon Kenny Wongchamcharoen Affiliation: University of California, Berkeley Affiliation: Berkeley, CA 94720 Email: pattaraphon.kenny@berkeley.edu    Paul Glasserman Affiliation: Columbia Business School Affiliation: New York, NY 10027 Email: pg20@gsb.columbia.edu\nL108: Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in finance and economics, where prompt-based attempts against look-ahead bias implicitly assume that models understand chronology. We test this fundamental question with a series of chronological ordering tasks with increasing complexities over facts the model already knows from pre-training. Our tasks cover (1) chronological ordering, (2) conditional sorting (filter, then order), and (3) anachronism detection.\nL109: We evaluate GPT-4.1 and Claude-3.7 Sonnet, with and without Extended Thinking (ET), and the newly released GPT-5 across multiple reasoning-effort settings. Across models, Exact match rate drops sharply as sequences lengthen even while rank correlations stay high as LLMs largely preserve local order but struggle to maintain a single globally consistent timeline.\nL110: In conditional sorting, most failures stem from the filtering step rather than the ordering step, but GPT-5 and Claude-3.7 with Extended Thinking outshine normal models significantly. Lastly, anachronism detection is found to be the easiest task for the LLMs but performance still declines with increasingly overlapping timelines or entities.\nL111: Overall, our main significant contribution is showing that allocating explicit reasoning budget helps with chronological ordering with GPT-5 at medium/high reasoning effort achieving flawless ordering at all lengths and perfect conditional sorting (both self-filtered and given-subset), whereas low/minimal effort degrades with longer lists, mirroring earlier models.\nL112: We release all code and evaluation templates to support full reproducibility.^{1}^{1} 1 This manuscript is an extended version of our work presented at the AAAI-26 AI4TS Workshop (poster) and the AAAI-26 Student Abstract Program (oral). The code repository is available at: cite92†https://github.com/kennywong524/chronollm†github.com ”\nL113: ## 1 Introduction\nL114: Large language models (LLMs) have shown great potential as forecasting tools for finance and economics. Typical applications ask LLMs to predict the direction of stock prices based on news reports, company earnings calls, or analysts’ research.\nL115: Yet it has also been recognized that the backtesting of LLM forecasts is subject to look-ahead bias ((cite93†Glasserman & Lin, 2024 ), cite94†Sarkar & Vafa (2024) ) when the backtesting period overlaps with the LLMs training window: the LLM may be asked to predict an outcome it has already seen. Leakage of post-event information embedded in the LLM’s pre-training corpus can inflate estimated forecast performance and lead to disappointing out-of-sample results.\nL116: Various methods have been proposed to try to measure and mitigate this problem. The simplest approach is to limit testing to an LLM’s post-training period (as in, e.g., cite95†Halawi et al. (2024) , cite96†Lopez-Lira & Tang (2024) ), but this severely limits the testing window, particularly for the most recent models. cite93†Glasserman & Lin (2024) find that masking company names in news headlines can be effective in removing look-ahead bias; this idea has been extended to larger texts in cite97†Engelberg et al.\nL202: Figure 1: Event-knowledge filtering pipeline\nL204: To contextualize performance, we benchmarked GPT-4.1 against $1{,}000$ uniformly random permutations per list length and expressed model performance as an empirical percentile relative to this distribution. Across all list sizes GPT-4.1 ranks in the 95th–100th percentile for rank-correlation and normalized Cayley distance, confirming a substantial advantage over random guessing.\nL210: All ordering experiments used a deterministic event shuffle and temperature fixed at zero (temp = 0) for our API calls. Even so, re-submitting the exact same prompt occasionally produced small differences in the ranked list—behavior commonly reported for current LLM APIs (due to their non-deterministic nature) and not specific to our setting.\nL211: To accommodate this, we ran two repeats per trial and report evaluation metrics (Spearman’s $\\rho$, Kendall’s $\\tau$, $\\text{Cayley}_{\\mathrm{norm}}$) averaged across repeats; our qualitative results and conclusions are unchanged.\nL212: ### 4.1 Findings\nL213: ##### 20th Century Historical Timeline Result\nL214: \nL215: We conducted 20 trials for each list size according to the methodology described in Figure 【106†1 . Table cite107†1 shows the aggregate result for each list size, $n$.\nL216: Table 1: Aggregated performance on the filtered corpus (20 trials per list size).\nL217: $n_{\\text{events}}$  | $\\bar{\\rho}$  | $\\bar{\\tau}$  | Normalized Cayley  | Cayley  | Exact match rate\nL218: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL219: $2$  | $1.000$  | $1.000$  | $0.000$  | $0.00$  | $1.00$\nL220: $5$  | $0.940$  | $0.880$  | $0.150$  | $0.60$  | $0.45$\nL221: $10$  | $0.955$  | $0.876$  | $0.267$  | $2.40$  | $0.10$\nL222: $20$  | $0.963$  | $0.869$  | $0.418$  | $7.95$  | $0.00$\nL223: $50$  | $0.928$  | $0.808$  | $0.762$  | $37.35$  | $0.00$\nL361: #### 6.3.1 Key findings\nL469: Each instance is labeled $\\,y\\in\\{0,1\\}$ as $\\text{Possible}=0$ or $\\text{Not\\,possible}=1$. Predictions $\\hat{y}$ are obtained from the model’s string outputs after regex pattern capture. Precision/recall treat $1$ (Not possible) as the positive class.\nL470: Let $n$ be the number of unique (deduplicated) instances, $\\text{TP}=\\sum\\mathbbm{1}[y{=}1,\\hat{y}{=}1]$, $\\text{FP}=\\sum\\mathbbm{1}[y{=}0,\\hat{y}{=}1]$, $\\text{TN}=\\sum\\mathbbm{1}[y{=}0,\\hat{y}{=}0]$, and $\\text{FN}=\\sum\\mathbbm{1}[y{=}1,\\hat{y}{=}0]$. We report:\nL476: ##### Variant 1: Single–boundary feasibility\nL477: \nL478: Table 6: Variant 1 GPT-4.1 Anachronism Detection Results by Batch Size and Type\nL479: Batch Size  | Experiment Type  | Metrics (After Deduplication)\nL480: --- | --- | ---\nL481:  |  | Accuracy  | Precision  | Recall  | F1 Score\nL482: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL483: N = 6  | 3_true_3_false  | 0.998  | 1.000  | 1.000  | 1.000\nL484: 6_false  | 0.995  | N/A  | N/A  | N/A\nL485: 6_true  | 0.995  | 1.000  | 0.974  | 0.987\nL486: N = 10  | 10_false  | 0.950  | N/A  | N/A  | N/A\nL487: 10_true  | 0.908  | 1.000  | 0.890  | 0.942\nL488: 5_true_5_false  | 0.932  | 0.933  | 0.933  | 0.933\nL489: N = 20  | 10_true_10_false  | 0.992  | 1.000  | 0.989  | 0.994\nL490: 20_false  | 0.982  | N/A  | N/A  | N/A\nL594:  | $$\\text{EMR}\\;=\\;\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\mathbb{I}\\!\\bigl[\\hat{\\sigma}^{(t)}=\\sigma^{(t)}\\bigr].$$  |\nL595: \nL596: It equals the proportion of trials in which the predicted list is identical to the ground truth and, equivalently, the Cayley distance is zero.\nL597: #### A.1.1 Position-wise error metric\nL598: ##### Mean Absolute Rank Difference (MARD)\nL599: \nL600: Let trials be indexed by $t$ with list length $n_{t}$. For an item $e$ in trial $t$, denote its true and predicted ranks by $r_{\\text{true}}^{(t)}(e),\\hat{r}^{(t)}(e)\\in\\{1,\\dots,n_{t}\\}$. Fix a list length $n$ and a ground-truth position $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$, and define\nL601: \nL602:  | $$\\mathcal{I}_{n,k}\\;=\\;\\bigl\\{(t,e):\\,n_{t}=n,\\;r_{\\text{true}}^{(t)}(e)=k\\bigr\\}.$$  |\nL603: \nL604: The mean absolute rank difference (MARD) at position $k$ is\nL605:  | $$\\mathrm{MARD}_{n}(k)\\;=\\;\\frac{1}{\\lvert\\mathcal{I}_{n,k}\\rvert}\\sum_{(t,e)\\in\\mathcal{I}_{n,k}}\\bigl|\\,\\hat{r}^{(t)}(e)-k\\,\\bigr|.$$  |\nL606: \nL607: This measure takes values between 0 and $n-1$, with lower values indicating smaller errors.\nL608: ##### Per-position accuracy\nL609: \nL610: Let criteria $P$ specify the target set $G_{P}=\\{i_{1},\\dots,i_{n}\\}$ (e.g., ABC-First-Names, OhioOrVirginia), with ground-truth order $\\pi^{\\text{true}}=(i_{1},\\dots,i_{n})$. For trial $t$, let the model’s prediction be $\\pi^{(t)}=(i^{(t)}_{1},\\dots,i^{(t)}_{m_{t}})$. We condition on the subset of trials with no length mismatch:\nL611: \nL612:  | $$\\mathcal{T}_{n,P}\\;=\\;\\{\\,t:\\;m_{t}=n\\,\\}.$$  |\nL613: \nL614: For rank $r\\in\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$, define the exact-occupant accuracy\nL615:  | $$A_{n,P}(r)\\;=\\;\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{T}_{n,P}|}\\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal{T}_{n,P}}\\mathbf{1}\\!\\left[\\,i^{(t)}_{r}=i_{r}\\,\\right].$$  |\nL616: \nL617: This measure takes values between 0 and 1, with higher values indicating greater accuracy.\nL1024: #### A.7.1 GPT-4.1’s performance on 20th Century Historical Timeline\nL1197: Extras\nL1198: President  | Count  | Note\nL1199: Millard Fillmore  | 2  | appended\nL1200: James Buchanan  | 1  | appended\nL1201: Abraham Lincoln  | 1  | appended\nL1202: Andrew Johnson  | 1  | appended\nL1203: Missing\nL1204: William Henry Harrison  | 2  | omitted\nL1205: John Tyler  | 2  | omitted\nL1206: Zachary Taylor  | 1  | omitted\nL1207: James Madison  | 1  | omitted\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDo Large Language Models (LLMs) Understand Chronology? (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.14214v2)\nciteturn2341view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2340view1\",\"patte